<p>Our packers wrap thousands of items a year. These are the habits that stop things breaking and make unpacking fast.</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Start with the rooms you use least.</strong> Lofts, garages and spare rooms can be packed weeks ahead.</li>
    <li><strong>Heavy items in small boxes.</strong> Books and tins go in small boxes; bedding and cushions in large ones.</li>
    <li><strong>Fill every gap.</strong> Items that can move in a box can break. Use paper, towels or tea towels as filler.</li>
    <li><strong>Plates on their edge.</strong> Wrap individually and stand them like records; they are far stronger on edge than flat.</li>
    <li><strong>Glasses in cell boxes.</strong> Or wrap each in paper and pack upright with the heaviest at the bottom.</li>
    <li><strong>Tape the bottom twice.</strong> Run tape along the seam and across it in an H.</li>
    <li><strong>Label the side, not the top.</strong> Room, contents and "fragile" on two sides so labels stay visible when stacked.</li>
    <li><strong>Bag and tape fixings.</strong> Screws and bolts from dismantled furniture go in a labelled bag taped to the item.</li>
    <li><strong>Wardrobe cartons for clothes.</strong> Hang clothes straight in and they come out uncreased.</li>
    <li><strong>Photograph cable set-ups.</strong> A quick photo of the back of the TV saves an hour of guesswork.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep liquids separate.</strong> Seal caps with cling film and pack upright in a plastic box.</li>
    <li><strong>Pack an essentials box last.</strong> Kettle, chargers, toiletries, bedding and documents travel with you.</li>
</ol>
<p>Short on time? Our <a href="{{ route('services.show', 'packing') }}">packing service</a> brings everything and does it for you, usually the day before the move.</p>
